<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Description>
      The wire contract of reactive SignalR transfers that a client and a hub both compile against:
      the transfer metadata and protocol version, receipts, per-transfer options, the callbacks the hub
      makes on a client, and the sender and receiver a client application is written against.
      References neither the SignalR client nor the ASP.NET Core server.
    </Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The one SignalR assembly the contract names: HubException is how the sender reports a transfer that
    did not get through, so it is part of what IReactiveTransferSender promises. It is the assembly both
    the client and the server already share, and the ASP.NET Core shared framework replaces it on a host.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Common" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The hub's method names stay out of the public surface. The client invokes them by nameof and the hub
    implements them, so a rename on one side stops compiling on the other — but which calls the wire
    carries is how the two packages talk, not something a consumer calls or implements. Public to internal
    is a breaking change and internal to public is not, so the reversible direction is the one taken.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.SignalR.Reactive.Client" />
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Snail.Toolkit.SignalR.Reactive.Server" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
